  "topicName": "11. Exercise 2.1",
  "explanation": "Exercise 2.1 focuses on negative integral exponents. Key rules: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ; (a/b)⁻ⁿ = (b/a)ⁿ; a⁰ = 1 for a ≠ 0; a⁻¹ = 1/a. When adding fractions from negative exponents, find a common denominator. Exponents cannot be combined across addition — only across multiplication (product law) or division (quotient law).",
  "examples": [
    "2⁻³ = 1/2³ = 1/8",
    "(2/3)⁻² = (3/2)² = 9/4",
    "3⁻¹ + 4⁻¹ = 1/3 + 1/4 = 7/12"
  ],
  "brainstorming": [
    {
      "question": "What does a negative exponent mean?",
      "answer": "It means take the reciprocal: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. It does NOT make the answer negative."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can you add exponents when adding terms like 3⁻¹ + 4⁻¹?",
      "answer": "No. Convert to fractions first, then add. You cannot write 7⁻¹."
    }
  ],
  "workedProblems": [
